# Hand history fixtures are byte-exact evidence, not source code.
#
# Real exports ship with CRLF, bare CR, UTF-16LE and cp1252 bytes, and the
# parsers exist precisely to survive that. Git's autocrlf normalisation
# (core.autocrlf=input is set on at least one machine here) rewrites CRLF to
# LF on commit, which silently turns a CRLF test case into an LF one while the
# working tree still looks correct. Mark them binary so the stored blob is the
# original bytes.
fixtures/** -text -diff
gg-hh/** -text -diff
weplay-hh/** -text -diff
tests/test/fixtures/** -text -diff

# The golden oracle (testdata/golden/README.md, docs/RAIL-PLAN.md R0.3) is
# generated text that the Rust core must reproduce byte for byte, on Windows
# too. Unlike the fixtures it is our own output, so it stays diffable - but it
# is always LF, whatever core.autocrlf says on the machine that checks it out.
testdata/golden/** text eol=lf
# The stats goldens (R2.1) likewise.
testdata/golden-stats/** text eol=lf
# And the all-in EV goldens (R2.2).
testdata/golden-ev/** text eol=lf

# TypeScript declarations generated from the Rust PHF model (docs/RAIL-PLAN.md
# R0.4). A cargo test compares them byte for byte with what the model renders,
# so they are LF on every checkout, Windows included.
core/rail-core-wasm/types/*.d.ts text eol=lf
